    <summary>Compress any video to an exact size, locally, with a measured quality report.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
Compress a video to a size you choose, on your own machine, and see what the compression cost you.

`halfsize` computes a bitrate budget from the target size, picks the largest resolution that budget can carry, runs two-pass x264, then measures VMAF and SSIM against the source. Nothing is uploaded, there is no account, no quota and no watermark.

```
npx halfsize input.mp4 --to 20mb
npx halfsize input.mp4 --for discord
npx halfsize input.mp4 --for whatsapp
npx halfsize --web
```

```
136 MB  ->  19.45 MB   7.0x smaller   2.7% under target
VMAF 88.3   SSIM 0.971   slight softening
```

### Why this package exists

The usual advice is `ffmpeg -crf 28`. That trades quality for size and leaves the size wherever it lands, which is useless when a form says 20 MB. Hitting a number means budgeting the bitrate, encoding twice and checking the result. That is what this tool automates.

### What you get

* Target size in MB, MiB or bytes, verified after encoding (lands 1-3% under the cap).
* Automatic downscale: 1080p when the budget allows it, 720p or 540p when it does not.
* Platform shortcuts: `--for discord`, `--for whatsapp`, `--for wechat`, `--for email`, `--for outlook`, `--for twitter`.
* VMAF and SSIM measured against your source, printed as a plain-language verdict.
* A local web UI (`halfsize --web`) if you prefer a button.
* Batch mode, `--json` output, `--dry-run`, `--codec h265`, `--doctor`.
* `halfsize --fix` downloads FFmpeg and verifies it against the published sha256 when the npm install skipped it.
* `halfsize --mcp` exposes a single `compress_video` tool to MCP clients such as Claude Desktop or Cursor.

### Notes

* Nothing else is required: the package downloads a ZIP that already contains ffmpeg.exe and ffprobe.exe. No Node.js, no first-run download, no installer.
* Everything runs locally. Your video never leaves the machine.
    ]]></description>
    </metadata>
</package>
